# Where to put these:
#
# • Vercel (Production + Preview + Development): DATABASE_URL* / POSTGRES_* / NEON_* — usually
#   injected by the Neon integration or `vercel env pull`. Also set JWT_SECRET (and optional
#   ADMIN_SETUP_SECRET) in Project → Settings → Environment Variables, or via API/CLI.
#
# • Local machine — pick ONE style (do not commit secrets):
#   - `.env.development.local` — Vite + `vercel dev` / `vercel env pull` (gitignored)
#   - `.env` — same, if you prefer; keep it gitignored
#
# • Seed script only (local): DEFAULT_ADMIN_EMAIL / DEFAULT_ADMIN_PASSWORD — not needed on Vercel.
#
# • Never commit: passwords, DATABASE_URL with credentials, JWT_SECRET, VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN.

# Neon — pooled URL recommended for serverless (see Neon dashboard).
# On Vercel, the Neon/Postgres integration may set POSTGRES_URL only; the API accepts DATABASE_URL,
# POSTGRES_URL, or POSTGRES_PRISMA_URL (first one wins).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST/DB?sslmode=require

# API auth — min 16 characters; must match between local API and Vercel serverless
JWT_SECRET=your-long-random-secret-here

# Optional: enables POST /api/auth/register with body { email, password, setupSecret }
ADMIN_SETUP_SECRET=

# Optional: Unsplash API access key for admin "daily challenge" random photo (https://unsplash.com/oauth/applications).
# Without it, the API uses built-in fallback landscape images (still Unsplash-hosted; no API quota).
UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY=

# One-time seed (pnpm db:seed) — local only. Scripts load .env → .env.local → .env.development.local
# (same idea as Vite; put secrets in .env.development.local if you use that file).
DEFAULT_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@yourdomain.com
DEFAULT_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Add another admin user (run once per user; do not commit real values):
# NEW_USER_EMAIL=someone@example.com NEW_USER_PASSWORD='your-password' pnpm db:add-user
#
# Debug “login works in DB but not in browser”: same DATABASE_URL as API, then:
# VERIFY_EMAIL=you@example.com VERIFY_PASSWORD='your-password' pnpm db:verify-login

# Local dev — easiest: `pnpm dev` (API :3002 + Vite :5173). Set PORT to move the API
# port when running several apps at once, e.g. `PORT=3005 pnpm dev`; the Vite proxy follows it.
# It sets VITE_USE_LOCAL_API=1 so
# `VITE_API_BASE_URL` is ignored and /api is proxied to localhost (avoids CORS to production).
# Or two terminals: pnpm dev:vercel  then  pnpm dev — for that, comment out VITE_API_BASE_URL below.
# Or hit the live site’s API (no vercel dev). Live domain:
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://cyansphotos.com
# VITE_API_PROXY_TARGET=http://127.0.0.1:3002

# Production on https://cyansphotos.com: leave VITE_API_BASE_URL unset so /api is same-origin.

# PostHog browser analytics — public values injected into the Vite bundle.
# Set VITE_POSTHOG_KEY on Vercel for Production, Preview AND Development, not
# just locally: a build without it ships an app that captures nothing.
VITE_POSTHOG_KEY=phc_your_public_project_token

# VITE_POSTHOG_HOST is an OPTIONAL override. Leave it unset in production.
#
# Each site sends events to its own first-party reverse proxy, taken from
# `analyticsHost` in config/sites.ts (t.addisonsphotos.com / t.cyansphotos.com),
# so the host follows VITE_SITE automatically. Being on the site's own domain is
# what keeps tracking blockers from dropping the requests.
#
# Set this only to point somewhere else — local development against PostHog
# directly, or to bypass a proxy that is misbehaving without a code change.
# VITE_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com

# PostHog source map upload — BUILD TIME ONLY, never VITE_-prefixed. A VITE_
# prefix would inline the personal key into the browser bundle for anyone to read.
#
# POSTHOG_API_KEY is a *personal* API key beginning with phx_ (Settings →
# Personal API keys, "Source map upload" preset). It is NOT the public phc_
# project token above — they are different keys with different jobs.
#
# Without these the build still succeeds; it just skips the upload and
# production stack traces stay minified. Set them on the Vercel project so
# every deploy uploads maps for that exact build.
POSTHOG_API_KEY=
POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID=
POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com

# Admin image uploads — file bytes go to Vercel Blob; Neon only stores the public URL on each photo row.
# Without this token, POST /api/upload returns 503 ("Upload storage is not configured").
#
# --- Option A: Vercel CLI (from repo root, project linked via `vercel link`) ---
# 1. pnpm vercel:blob:create -- cyan-photography-blob
#    (pick any unused store name). When prompted: link to this project = Yes.
#    Then select Production, Preview, and Development (space toggles, Enter confirms).
# 2. pnpm vercel:env:pull   → writes Development env (incl. BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN) to .env.development.local
# 3. Redeploy production on Vercel so Preview/Production pick up the new env var if not already.
#
# --- Option B: Dashboard ---
# Vercel → Project → Storage → Blob → Create / link store → env var BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN on the project.
# Local: copy the token into .env.development.local or run `pnpm vercel:env:pull`.
#
# Docs: https://vercel.com/docs/storage/vercel-blob | CLI: https://vercel.com/docs/cli/blob
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=

# Optional: comma-separated extra origins for /api CORS (defaults include localhost:5173 + cyansphotos.com)
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5174,https://staging.example.com

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which site this deployment serves. One codebase, several portfolios — the
# branding for each lives in config/sites.ts. Valid values: addison | cyan.
#
#   VITE_SITE  → baked into the browser bundle at build time (title, favicon,
#                manifest, hero wordmark, footer, Instagram link)
#   SITE       → read at runtime by the serverless functions (CORS origins,
#                transactional email From address, seed admin address)
#
# Both must be set, to the same value, on every Vercel environment.
VITE_SITE=addison
SITE=addison

# Transactional email (password reset) via Resend. The From address comes from
# config/sites.ts and its domain must be verified in Resend.
# Without this, POST /api/auth/forgot-password returns 503.
# https://resend.com/api-keys
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Google sign-in for the admin. A Google account can only sign in when its
# verified address already has a user row — Google authenticates, it never
# enrols a new admin.
#
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID       → server-side audience check on the ID token
#   VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID  → client-side button; unset hides the button entirely
#
# Both are the same OAuth *web* client id (the client secret is not needed for
# the ID-token flow). Every origin the admin is served from must be listed under
# "Authorized JavaScript origins" in Google Cloud Console — including the www
# host if the apex redirects to it.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Site provisioning — the Sites panel in the admin. Owner-only.
#
# VERCEL_TOKEN is an account- or team-scoped token; Vercel cannot scope one to a
# single project, so it reaches every project on the account. Prefer a token on
# a team that holds only these sites. Nothing in this app deletes projects.
#
# OWNER_EMAIL is the one account allowed to manage sites. Provisioning creates
# infrastructure, so it is not granted to every admin who can sign in.
#
# SITES_REPO separates "a site" from the unrelated projects sharing the account:
# every site is a deployment of this repository. On Vercel it is derived
# automatically from VERCEL_GIT_REPO_OWNER / VERCEL_GIT_REPO_SLUG, so it is only
# needed locally. Unset and underivable, the panel lists nothing rather than
# listing everything.
VERCEL_TOKEN=
VERCEL_TEAM_ID=
OWNER_EMAIL=dallaspeters@gmail.com
SITES_REPO=dallasbpeters/photo-portfolio
